  "audience": "engineers and researchers who know what a language model is and have not read the note; in depth on the loop and on what makes a critic, so the agent tasks and the public benchmarks are left out of three minutes",
  "form": "2d",
  "why": [
   "an argument about text checked against text, then counts out of 104: each claim stands beside its evidence (the models' own answers, quoted on sheets) and every count is a strip of 104 cells on one scale; nothing here has depth or parts, and a quoted answer has to be read flat, so 2D",
   "one desk and two chairs, then the chairs swapped: the same two models give the note's best and its worst result, so the order is catch, damage, count, cause, repair, the larger gain, limits, not the note's section order; the look is a fact-checker's desk: desk green, proof paper, one red pencil for every objection, a highlighter, Literata (made for reading documents on Android) for what the models wrote and Roboto for what the lab says about it"
